<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">The right answer depends on the computer, the fault, the software you use and the value of your time. Compare the total quoted cost of the work with the cost of a suitable replacement, including setup and moving your files. Ask what the proposed change is expected to improve and what it will leave unresolved. There is no single birthday at which every computer must be replaced.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">A cheap part is not automatically a good investment if the rest of the system is near the end of its useful life. Conversely, replacement may be unnecessary when a straightforward, safe adjustment solves the actual problem.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the delay happens when switching on or signing in, read our <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/computer-slow-startup/">guide to a computer that takes a long time to start</a>.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">If the delay is mainly online, our <a class="font-semibold text-accent-ink underline underline-offset-4 hover:text-ink" href="/guides/slow-computer-or-internet/">computer or internet comparison guide</a> helps you describe where the waiting starts.</p>
<h2 class="mt-12 mb-5 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink md:text-3xl">If you only have ten minutes</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Start by describing one real task that feels slow: opening email, joining a video call or finding a document. Note whether the delay happens before the computer is ready, inside one program or only when using the internet. Those are different starting points for a conversation, even when the experience feels equally frustrating.</p>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">The useful outcome is a clear next step. You should be able to understand what was checked, what was changed, what remains uncertain and whether more work would need a separate quote.</p>
<h2 class="mt-12 mb-5 text-2xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink md:text-3xl">A practical checklist</h2>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Before arranging help, note what you can. You do not need to answer every question or diagnose the computer yourself:</p>
<ul class="mb-6 list-disc space-y-3 pl-6 text-muted">
<li>When did the slowdown begin?</li>
<li>Is it present at startup, while opening programs, or during a particular task?</li>
<li>Does restarting make a temporary difference?</li>
<li>How much storage appears to be available?</li>
<li>Which programs do you use most often?</li>
<li>Has the computer shown unusual messages, crashes or hardware noise?</li>
<li>Are there important files or accounts that need special care?</li>
<li>Do you need the computer for work, study, communication or another time-sensitive purpose?</li>
</ul>
<p class="mb-5 leading-relaxed text-muted">Do not assume that every fault can be fixed in one visit. Some work may require parts, account recovery, data transfer, a new setup, provider assistance, workshop time or follow-up. Those are separate considerations from an initial performance assessment.</p>
